# smoke-test.sh configuration for terraform-kubernetes-n8n.
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your values.
# .env is gitignored, never commit your API key.

# ── Terraform directory ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Path to the Terraform directory to read state from.
# Defaults to the current working directory, so running the script from inside
# examples/homelab (or your own root configuration) just works without setting
# this. Override if running the script from a different location.
# TERRAFORM_DIR=/path/to/examples/homelab

# ── Auto-detected from Terraform (override here if needed) ────────────────────
# These are read automatically from `terraform output` when TERRAFORM_DIR has
# a terraform.tfstate. Only set them here to explicitly override.

# Base URL of your n8n deployment (no trailing slash).
# N8N_URL=https://n8n.example.com

# Kubernetes namespace n8n is deployed into.
# N8N_NAMESPACE=n8n

# ── Routing requests to the ingress directly ───────────────────────────────────
# When the hostname is a real public record in front of the cluster, for
# example the Cloudflare examples' proxied CNAMEs to a tunnel, and the editor
# Ingress carries an IP allowlist, ordinary requests reach the allowlist from
# the tunnel's egress address instead of your network and get rejected with a
# 403 on an otherwise healthy deployment. Set this to a literal IP address
# from the Ingress status; a cloud load balancer may show a hostname there
# instead (kubectl get ingress -n n8n), resolve it first (e.g. dig +short
# <hostname>) to route every request there directly while
# keeping the real Host header and TLS SNI. Unset by default.
# SMOKE_RESOLVE_IP=203.0.113.10

# ── Required for API and workflow execution tests ─────────────────────────────
# n8n API key, create one in n8n: Settings > API > Create API Key
N8N_API_KEY=your-api-key-here

# ── Optional load-scaling test ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set to "true" to fire CPU-burning workflow executions and confirm KEDA
# (or the worker HPA, if configured) scales workers up.
# LOAD_TEST=false

# Number of webhook executions to fire during the load test (default: 100).
# LOAD_REQUESTS=100

# How long each worker job burns CPU in seconds (default: 10).
# Increase to 60+ if workers aren't saturating before the autoscaler reacts.
# LOAD_JOB_DURATION_SECS=10

# Seconds to wait for the autoscaler to react after load is applied (default: 180).
# KEDA / HPA have built-in stabilization windows, scaling won't appear instantly.
# SCALE_WAIT_SECS=180

# ── verify-custom-image.sh only ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# These are ignored by smoke-test.sh. See the README section "Custom image
# verification" for what each one enables.

# Path to a workflow JSON that uses one of your baked nodes. Enables the
# end-to-end execution check, which is the only thing that proves a *worker*
# resolved the custom node type rather than just the main process. The workflow
# needs a webhook trigger and a node whose type starts with "CUSTOM.".
# CUSTOM_NODE_WORKFLOW=./my-node-test.json

# Assert the deployed image repository matches n8n_image_repository exactly.
# EXPECT_IMAGE_REPOSITORY=registry.example.com/n8n

# Assert N8N_CUSTOM_EXTENSIONS matches n8n_custom_extensions_path exactly.
# EXPECT_EXTENSIONS_PATH=/opt/n8n-nodes
